### What is AOP and why does it exist?

AOP stands for Aspect-Oriented Programming. The whole idea is to pull cross-cutting concerns out of your business code. A cross-cutting concern is something that almost every method needs but that has nothing to do with the actual business logic. Examples include measuring how long a method took, writing an audit entry, checking security permissions, and managing database transactions.

Without AOP, Rajesh would copy the same timing code into two hundred methods at National Bank of India. With AOP, he writes that timing logic once in an Aspect, and Spring applies it to all those methods automatically.

```java
// WITHOUT AOP: timing code repeated in every method
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {

    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();       // timing boilerplate
        try {
            // actual business logic
            debit(from, amount);
            credit(to, amount);
            auditLog(from, to, amount);
        } finally {
            long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            log.info("transfer took {} ms", duration);  // timing boilerplate
        }
    }
    // Same timing boilerplate in calculateInterest(), processLoan(), etc.
}

// WITH AOP: timing logic written once, applied everywhere
// TransferService contains ONLY business logic
// TimingAspect handles timing for all methods annotated with @LogExecutionTime
```

### How does AOP work via proxies?

When Spring sees that a bean needs an aspect applied, it does not hand your code the real object. Instead, it wraps the real object inside a proxy object that looks identical from the outside. Every call from outside the object goes to the proxy first. The proxy runs the aspect logic, then delegates to the real method, then runs any after logic, and returns the result.

There are two kinds of proxy:

| Proxy Type | When Used | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| JDK Dynamic Proxy | Bean implements an interface | Creates a runtime class implementing the same interface |
| CGLIB Proxy | Bean has no interface | Generates a subclass of your class at runtime |

Spring Boot defaults to CGLIB proxies for most beans.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

@Service
public class InterestService {

    // When AOP is active, Spring does NOT give you this object directly.
    // It gives you a CGLIB proxy that wraps this object.

    @LogExecutionTime   // custom annotation, aspect intercepts this
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(String accountId, BigDecimal principal, int months) {
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.065");
        return principal.multiply(rate)
            .multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(months))
            .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(12), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}
```

```text
Caller -> CGLIB Proxy -> @Before advice -> Real Method -> @After advice -> Return to caller

The caller never touches the real InterestService object.
The proxy intercepts every call and runs the aspect logic transparently.
```

### What are the five advice types?

Each advice type runs at a different point relative to the target method.

| Advice | Runs When | Receives | Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@Before` | Before the method | JoinPoint | Security check, logging entry |
| `@After` | After (success or exception) | JoinPoint | Cleanup, like finally |
| `@AfterReturning` | After successful return | JoinPoint + return value | Audit the result |
| `@AfterThrowing` | After exception | JoinPoint + exception | Error logging, alerting |
| `@Around` | Wraps the method completely | ProceedingJoinPoint | Timing, caching, retry, circuit breaker |

```java
package com.bank.aspect;

import org.aspectj.lang.JoinPoint;
import org.aspectj.lang.ProceedingJoinPoint;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.After;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.AfterReturning;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.AfterThrowing;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.Around;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.Aspect;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.Before;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Aspect
@Component
public class TransferAuditAspect {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TransferAuditAspect.class);

    @Before("execution(* com.bank.service.TransferService.transfer(..))")
    public void beforeTransfer(JoinPoint jp) {
        log.info("Starting transfer: {}", java.util.Arrays.toString(jp.getArgs()));
    }

    @AfterReturning(pointcut = "execution(* com.bank.service.TransferService.transfer(..))",
                    returning = "result")
    public void afterTransfer(JoinPoint jp, Object result) {
        log.info("Transfer completed successfully");
    }

    @AfterThrowing(pointcut = "execution(* com.bank.service.TransferService.transfer(..))",
                   throwing = "ex")
    public void onTransferError(JoinPoint jp, Exception ex) {
        log.error("Transfer failed: {}", ex.getMessage());
    }
}
```

### How do you create a custom annotation with @Around?

`@Around` is the most powerful advice type because it completely surrounds the method. It decides what happens before, decides whether to call the real method at all, can modify the arguments, can modify the return value, and runs code after. For timing, you must use `@Around` because you need to record a start time, let the method run, and record an end time.

Step 1: Define a custom annotation.

```java
package com.bank.annotation;

import java.lang.annotation.ElementType;
import java.lang.annotation.Retention;
import java.lang.annotation.RetentionPolicy;
import java.lang.annotation.Target;

@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface LogExecutionTime {
}
```

Step 2: Write the `@Around` advice.

```java
package com.bank.aspect;

import com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime;
import org.aspectj.lang.ProceedingJoinPoint;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.Around;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.Aspect;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Aspect
@Component
public class TimingAspect {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TimingAspect.class);

    @Around("@annotation(com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime)")
    public Object measureTime(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            Object result = joinPoint.proceed();   // call the real method
            return result;
        } finally {
            long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            log.info("{}.{} took {} ms",
                joinPoint.getTarget().getClass().getSimpleName(),
                joinPoint.getSignature().getName(),
                duration);
        }
    }
}
```

Step 3: Apply the annotation to any method.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class LoanService {

    @LogExecutionTime
    public BigDecimal calculateEMI(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int months) {
        // Pure business logic, no timing boilerplate
        BigDecimal monthlyRate = rate.divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(12), 10,
            java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
        BigDecimal power = BigDecimal.ONE.add(monthlyRate).pow(months);
        return principal.multiply(monthlyRate).multiply(power)
            .divide(power.subtract(BigDecimal.ONE), 2, java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}
```

```text
INFO LoanService.calculateEMI took 3 ms
```

### What is the self-invocation pitfall?

The single most important consequence of proxy-based AOP is self-invocation. If a method inside your class calls another method of the same class directly using `this`, that call does not go through the proxy, so the aspect does not run. The proxy only intercepts calls that come in from outside the object.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    // WRONG: self-invocation bypasses the proxy
    public void processMonthEnd(String accountId) {
        // This call goes through "this", NOT through the proxy
        // @LogExecutionTime will NOT work
        this.calculateInterest(accountId);
    }

    @LogExecutionTime
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(String accountId) {
        // This method's timing will NOT be logged when called from processMonthEnd
        return new BigDecimal("1250.00");
    }
}

// CORRECT: extract to a separate bean so the call goes through the proxy
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class InterestCalculationService {

    @LogExecutionTime
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(String accountId) {
        return new BigDecimal("1250.00");
        // Now the proxy intercepts this call because it comes from outside
    }
}

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final InterestCalculationService interestService;

    public AccountService(InterestCalculationService interestService) {
        this.interestService = interestService;
    }

    public void processMonthEnd(String accountId) {
        // This call goes through the CGLIB proxy of InterestCalculationService
        // @LogExecutionTime WORKS correctly
        interestService.calculateInterest(accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Self-invocation (WRONG):
  AccountService.processMonthEnd() --this.calculateInterest()--> real method
  Proxy is bypassed, @LogExecutionTime has no effect

Separate bean (CORRECT):
  AccountService --proxy--> InterestCalculationService.calculateInterest()
  Proxy intercepts, @LogExecutionTime works
```

### What is the difference between Filter, HandlerInterceptor, and OncePerRequestFilter?

These three are often confused but they operate at different levels in the request lifecycle.

| Aspect | Servlet Filter | HandlerInterceptor | OncePerRequestFilter |
|---|---|---|---|
| Level | Servlet container | Spring MVC | Servlet container |
| Runs | Before DispatcherServlet | After handler mapping | Before DispatcherServlet |
| Knows handler | No | Yes | No |
| Use case | Auth, CORS, logging | Timing, audit context | Same as Filter, guaranteed once |
| Forward behavior | May run twice on forward | Runs once per dispatch | Guaranteed once |

```java
// Servlet Filter: runs at the container level, before DispatcherServlet
package com.bank.filter;

import jakarta.servlet.Filter;
import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletResponse;
import java.io.IOException;

public class RequestLoggingFilter implements Filter {
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response,
                         FilterChain chain) throws IOException, jakarta.servlet.ServletException {
        // Runs BEFORE DispatcherServlet -- no idea which controller will handle this
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}

// HandlerInterceptor: runs inside Spring MVC, after handler mapping
package com.bank.interceptor;

import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.HandlerInterceptor;

public class AuditInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {
    @Override
    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,
                             Object handler) {
        // Runs AFTER handler mapping -- knows which controller method will be called
        return true;  // return false to block the request
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with AOP?

Besides self-invocation, the most common AOP mistakes involve pointcut expressions and exception handling.

```java
// WRONG: @Around advice that swallows exceptions
@Around("@annotation(com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime)")
public Object badAdvice(ProceedingJoinPoint jp) {
    try {
        return jp.proceed();
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        log.error("Error", t);
        return null;    // WRONG: caller receives null instead of exception
    }
}

// CORRECT: re-throw the exception after logging
@Around("@annotation(com.bank.annotation.LogExecutionTime)")
public Object goodAdvice(ProceedingJoinPoint jp) throws Throwable {
    try {
        return jp.proceed();
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        log.error("Error in {}", jp.getSignature().getName(), t);
        throw t;    // CORRECT: caller sees the real exception
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `@Transactional` is implemented as AOP advice. The self-invocation pitfall applies: calling a `@Transactional` method from within the same class bypasses the transaction proxy, so no transaction is started.
- Multiple aspects on the same method execute in order controlled by `@Order`. Lower values run first (outer layer of the onion). For National Bank of India, Pankaj orders: security check (@Order(1)) runs first, then transaction (@Order(2)), then timing (@Order(3)).
- CGLIB proxies cannot intercept `final` methods because CGLIB works by subclassing, and `final` methods cannot be overridden.
- AspectJ load-time weaving (LTW) is an alternative to Spring proxies. It modifies bytecode at class-load time, which means self-invocation works and `final` methods can be intercepted. It is more complex to set up.

```java
package com.bank.aspect;

import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.Aspect;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Aspect
@Component
@Order(1)   // runs first (outermost)
public class SecurityAspect { /* ... */ }

@Aspect
@Component
@Order(2)   // runs second
public class TransactionAspect { /* ... */ }

@Aspect
@Component
@Order(3)   // runs last (innermost)
public class TimingAspect { /* ... */ }
```

```text
Execution order for a method with all three aspects:
  SecurityAspect.before()
    TransactionAspect.before()
      TimingAspect.before()
        Real Method executes
      TimingAspect.after()
    TransactionAspect.after()
  SecurityAspect.after()
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the difference between `execution` and `@annotation` pointcuts? `execution` matches method signatures (package, class, method name, parameters). `@annotation` matches any method annotated with a specific annotation.
- Can AOP intercept private methods? No. Spring proxies work at the object boundary, and private methods are not accessible through proxies.
- What happens if `proceed()` is not called in `@Around` advice? The real method never executes. This can be used intentionally to short-circuit a call (for caching or authorization).
- How does `@Transactional` relate to AOP? `@Transactional` is implemented as an `@Around` advice that starts a transaction before the method and commits or rolls back after.
